{{!--
  The Failure Modes bullets shared by st-create-plan, st-generate-tasks,
  st-execute-blueprint and st-full-workflow. The call site owns the
  `## Failure Modes` heading and appends its own skill-specific bullets on the
  line immediately after the partial tag.

  The second bullet names both conditions because they are the same condition
  seen from two skills: st-create-plan allocates an ID and sees the script fail,
  while the other three resolve an existing ID and see it not resolve. Both are
  the script exiting non-zero, so one bullet covers them, but it has to say so.

  These two bullets previously existed as four copies whose wording had drifted:
  one forbade writing files, another forbade executing tasks, a third forbade
  both. The resolution is the union of what those copies forbid, so the shared
  bullet is at least as restrictive as the strictest copy it replaces. The
  disagreement is settled here, not turned into a hash arg — parameterizing it
  would encode the accident. A skill that genuinely needs different wording
  overrides at its own call site; this partial never gains a conditional.

  st-execute-task and st-refine-plan deliberately do not call this partial. Their
  no-root bullets forbid different things ("Do not execute the task." / "Do not
  read or write any plan files.") and both must keep rendering unchanged.
--}}- **No strikethroo root found.** Stop and instruct the user to initialize the
  project. Do not write any files or execute any tasks.
- **Plan ID does not resolve, or the plan-ID script fails.** Re-check the
  resolved root and re-run. If it continues to fail, surface the script's
  stderr to the user and stop. Do not guess an ID and do not write any files.
